<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hobart</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hobart is the capital of Tasmania, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, on the estuary of the Derwent River beneath Mount <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a>. Founded in 1804 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> Collins as a British penal settlement, it is Australia's second-oldest capital city after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney-nova-scotia">Sydney</a>. It grew as a whaling and shipping port and the administrative centre of Van Diemen's Land.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hobart.jpg" alt="Hobart" width="320" height="170" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michael fromholtz; derivative work by Georgfotoart · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Franklin_Wharf_2015_b_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Hobart lies in the south-east of Tasmania, on the estuary of the River Derwent, and is the southernmost capital city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. Its skyline is dominated by kunanyi / Mount Wellington, which rises to 1,271 metres, and its harbour is the second-deepest natural port in the world, with much of the waterfront built on reclaimed land. The city is built chiefly on Jurassic dolerite around the foothills. It has a cool to mild oceanic climate.</p><p>The land was known to the Muwinina people as nipaluna, a name that includes features such as kunanyi / Mount Wellington and the River Derwent. Before British colonisation it had been occupied, possibly for as long as 35,000 years, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aboriginal-tasmanians">Aboriginal Tasmanians</a>, who generally call themselves Palawa or Pakana.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first European settlement began in 1803 as a military camp at Risdon Cove on the eastern shore of the Derwent, amid British concern over French explorers. The camp was the site of the 1804 Risdon Cove massacre. Later in 1804 Captain David Collins moved the camp, together with soldiers, settlers and convicts from the abandoned Port Phillip settlement, to a better site at Sullivans Cove, the present site of Hobart. He named it Hobart Town, or Hobarton, after Lord Hobart, then British Secretary of State for war and the colonies. The city took the singular name Hobart in 1881.</p><p>Whaling soon became a major local industry, and for a time Hobart was the main whaling port of the Southern Ocean. Penal transportation ended in the 1850s, and the city then went through periods of growth and decline. A railway opened in 1876 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>'s first fully electric tram network in 1893, which allowed the urban area to spread. The early 20th century brought a boom based on mining, agriculture and other primary industries. The first general work of Australian literature to be published appeared in Hobart, and in 1824 Andrew Bent, proprietor of the Hobart Town Gazette, established the first free press in Australia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Hobart is the capital and most populous city of Tasmania and the seat of the Parliament of Tasmania. Greater Hobart had 247,068 people at the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, nearly half of the state's population. The city is the home port for Australian and French Antarctic operations.</p><p>Hobart is known for its well-preserved Georgian and Victorian buildings. The Theatre Royal, established in 1837 and designed by the colonial architect John Lee Archer, is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>'s oldest continually operating theatre. The Tasmanian Museum and Art Gallery was founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> of Tasmania. Salamanca Market and the Museum of Old and New Art, which opened in 2011, are well-known attractions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hobart” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Britain settled Van Diemen's Land in 1803 to forestall the French, and <b>Hobart</b> was founded in 1804.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-tasmania">Colony of Tasmania</a></li></ul></section>
